% A museum's entrance leads to either the courtyard or the gallery, and each
% of those routes can lead to either of two halls. A day-pass holder is
% guaranteed to reach the exhibit when every possible route leads there.
% Shows nested proof by cases with or in rule conclusions. A hall with an
% open door directly entails access to the exhibit.
% See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjunction_elimination

the templates are:
    *a visitor* holds a day pass,
    *a place* is open,
    *a place* leads to *a first destination* or to *a second destination*,
    *a place* entails access to the exhibit,
    *a visitor* is guaranteed to reach the exhibit.

the knowledge base museum routes includes:

% Each rule concludes a pair of alternatives. The west hall occurs in both
% branches, so the routes share part of their proof.
the entrance leads to the courtyard or to the gallery
    if the entrance is open.

the courtyard leads to the east hall or to the west hall
    if the courtyard is open.

the gallery leads to the west hall or to the atrium
    if the gallery is open.

% At a fork, access follows when both destinations entail it.
a place entails access to the exhibit
    if the place leads to a first destination or to a second destination
    and the first destination entails access to the exhibit
    and the second destination entails access to the exhibit.

a visitor is guaranteed to reach the exhibit
    if the visitor holds a day pass
    and the entrance entails access to the exhibit.

alex holds a day pass.
the entrance is open.
the courtyard is open.
the gallery is open.
the east hall entails access to the exhibit.
the west hall entails access to the exhibit.

scenario all doors open is:
    the atrium entails access to the exhibit.
    access expects answers ["alex is guaranteed to reach the exhibit"].

scenario atrium door closed is:
    access expects answers [].

query routes is:
    which place leads to which first destination or to which second destination.

query access is:
    alex is guaranteed to reach the exhibit.
